    sources: [{ id: "src_piccard_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Auguste Piccard (Q210719), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-15): English label 'Auguste Piccard', description 'Swiss physicist (1884-1962)', Korean label 오귀스트 피카르, human, born 28 January 1884, died 24 March 1962, citizen of Switzerland. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q210719" }, { id: "src_piccard_britannica", kind: "biography", title: "Pierre de Latil, 'Auguste Piccard', Encyclopaedia Britannica (read via the print edition of the article). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: Swiss-born Belgian physicist known for exploring both the upper stratosphere and the sea in vessels of his own design; studied at the Swiss Federal Institute of Technology in Zurich and took the new chair of applied physics at the University of Brussels in 1922; built a balloon in 1930 with Belgian financing to study cosmic rays, with an airtight pressurised cabin and a very large balloon that did not have to be fully filled at launch; reached 15,781 metres with Paul Kipfer on 27 May 1931 and flew again in 1932 with a new cabin and a radio; wanted since childhood to descend into the depths and designed the bathyscaphe, whose first unmanned trials in October and November 1948 left the float badly damaged; the project 'was subsequently troubled by various difficulties' until his son Jacques intervened and a new craft was built with Italian industry in 1953; retired in 1954. Short reference article, admiring in tone.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/print/article/459310" }, { id: "src_piccard_britannica_bathyscaphe", kind: "institution", title: "'Bathyscaphe', Encyclopaedia Britannica (read; last revised by Amy Tikkanen): the bathyscaphe was developed by Auguste Piccard, with help in later years from his son Jacques; the first, FNRS 2, was built in Belgium in 1946-1948 and damaged in the 1948 trials at the Cape Verde Islands, then rebuilt and improved as FNRS 3; the Trieste was launched on 1 August 1953 and dived to 3,150 metres that year; the United States Navy acquired it in 1958 and on 23 January 1960 Jacques Piccard and Lieutenant Don Walsh reached 10,916 metres in the Mariana Trench; a steel cabin under a gasoline-filled float. Encyclopedia article from the same publisher as the biography, so not an independent family.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/print/article/56002" }, { id: "src_piccard_ebsco_strato", kind: "biography", title: "Nancy M. Gordon, 'Piccard Travels to the Stratosphere by Balloon', EBSCO Research Starters, History (read in full, with its bibliography citing DeVorkin, Stehling and Piccard's own Earth, Sky, and Sea). Event article on the 27 May 1931 flight: his work from 1926 on a balloon and gondola for cosmic-ray research, the aluminium sealed gondola with a German submarine air-purifying apparatus, the partly inflated balloon and band suspension, Belgian FNRS funding, the September 1930 launch called off for weather, the premature release in 1931, a leak from a broken seal that he stopped with petroleum jelly mixed with fibres prepared for such an accident, the unusable gas valve, the landing on a Tyrolean glacier, the lost chance for most measurements and only one approximate cosmic-ray observation, the flight with Max Cosyns in August 1932 that filled in the altitude-intensity curve, and the later use of his cabin design. Encyclopedia-style event article that is frank about the limited scientific return.", url: "https://ebsco.com/research-starters/history/piccard-travels-stratosphere-balloon" }, { id: "src_piccard_cof", kind: "institution", title: "Linda Voss, 'The Race to the Stratosphere' (Record Balloon Flights), U.S. Centennial of Flight Commission (read in full): the death of Hawthorne Gray in 1927 after an open-basket flight, Piccard's 'absolute faith that science could solve anything', his sealed gondola using an apparatus developed by the Germans for submarines, the envelope five times larger than needed to take off, the 1931 and 1932 flights and the American and Soviet balloons that followed his example. Federal-commission history of high-altitude ballooning.", url: "https://centennialofflight.net/essay/Lighter_than_air/race_to_strato/LTA11.htm" }, { id: "src_piccard_history", kind: "press", title: "Kieran Mulvaney, 'The Nearly Fatal 1931 Balloon Journey to the Edge of Space', HISTORY (May 2026; read in full): the 1931 flight told largely from Piccard's own later writing, including the frozen gas valve, the broken gondola motor and rising heat, the leak sealed with petroleum jelly and cloth, the mercury sucked out through a hose, the 17-hour flight, the crash-landing on an Alpine glacier and the search party sent to recover bodies; the cosmic-ray dispute with Robert Millikan, and a claim that the 1932 data helped disprove Millikan's photon view. Popular history; the account of the flight rests on his own memoir, and the claim about a 1926 flight confirming Einstein is not used.", url: "https://www.history.com/articles/auguste-piccard-balloon-stratosphere" }, { id: "src_piccard_elhuyar", kind: "press", title: "Egoitz Etxebeste Aduriz, 'Piccard-en balentriak (I): laino guztien gainetik' (Piccard's feats I: above all the clouds), Elhuyar science magazine no. 269 (1 November 2010, in Basque; read in full): the sealed-cabin design that specialists called impossible because it had never been done (quoting his book Earth, Sky, and Sea), the test of the lead-shot ballast dropped from a chimney at Brussels, the premature 1931 release, the leak sealed with a prepared cotton and petroleum-jelly paste, the jammed valve cord, the heat and thirst, the glacier landing and the warm reception in Switzerland and Brussels, and the 1932 flight with Cosyns that 'went perfectly'. Popular-science article that draws its details from Piccard's own book, so it is a self-report family.", url: "https://zientzia.eus/artikuluak/piccard-en-balentriak-i-laino-guztien-gainetik/en" }, { id: "src_piccard_tdia", kind: "press", title: "Bryan R. Swopes, 'FNRS', This Day in Aviation (entry for 18 August 1932; read in full): the 1932 flight from Dubendorf with Max Cosyns to 16,201 metres, an FAI altitude record, funded by the Belgian FNRS; the aluminium gondola painted half white and half black for heat control, which did not work; the air-recycling system of the type used in submarines; the 1931 landing on the Gurgler Ferner glacier; honours from King Albert; nearly 30 ascents before he turned to the deep ocean. Aviation-history website citing FAI record files.", url: "https://www.thisdayinaviation.com/tag/fnrs/" }, { id: "src_piccard_stratocat", kind: "press", title: "'PICCARD, August A. (1884-1962)', Stratopedia, StratoCat balloon encyclopedia (read in full): twin brother Jean Felix, professor at Brussels from 1922, the spherical pressurised aluminium gondola funded by the Belgian FNRS, the 1931 flight with Kipfer from Augsburg and the 1932 flight with Max Cosyns from Dubendorf, the mid-1930s turn to a deep-sea craft begun in 1937 and interrupted by the Second World War, FNRS-2 resumed in 1945 with unmanned dives in 1948 before it was given to the French Navy in 1950, and a manned dive of 4,176 metres in 1954. Independent non-profit balloon-history site.", url: "https://stratocat.com.ar/stratopedia/128.htm" }],
