      // [src_paxton_dnb, src_paxton_lecture1850, src_paxton_britannica, src_paxton_curl | 1849-1854 | reference biography, self-account, reference article, reference entry] His Exhibition plans were prepared in about nine to ten days, the Exhibition building was finished in roughly six months (Britannica, Curl) and the Sydenham building re-erected in 1852-54. The six-month build was carried out by the contractors, and the quick design rested on a decade of earlier glasshouse work he describes himself. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 70 / 0.56 to 66 / 0.50 because the speed of building is shared credit and the ten-day figure comes from his own paper and the Dictionary of National Biography.
