      // [src_paxton_historicengland, src_paxton_curl, src_paxton_schoenefeldt, src_paxton_landow | 1832-1854 | heritage-body register entry, reference entry, architectural-history paper, modern biographical commentary] At Birkenhead Park he brought in Edward Kemp to supervise construction and John Robertson to design lodges (Historic England); Curl names Robertson's help on several works and a partnership with George Henry Stokes at Sydenham and Mentmore; Schoenefeldt records work with Henderson and Fox and the Chance Brothers glassmakers; and Landow, summarising Colquhoun, reports a close working friendship with the Duke of Devonshire and the support of Brunel and Stephenson for his plan. The sources say little about how he handled disagreements, and the same review notes his family bore a heavy cost, which is not scored here.
      collaboration: [68, 0.56, "s", "A"],
      // [src_paxton_dnb, src_paxton_curl, src_paxton_iln1865, src_paxton_landow | 1826-1865 | reference biography, reference entry, press obituary, modern biographical commentary] His work ran across horticulture and botanical publishing, estate management at Chatsworth, glasshouse engineering, village and house design (Edensor, Mentmore, Ferrieres), public parks and railway management (Dictionary of National Biography, Curl, 1865 obituary); Landow notes his railway-boom investments. Most of it grew out of a single base in gardening and building glass structures, which keeps the score moderate. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 64 / 0.52 to 60 / 0.50 because the breadth is mostly adjacent work built on one skill and none of the sources treats range as a distinct trait.
