    sources: [{ id: "src_tsiolkovsky_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Konstantin Tsiolkovsky (Q41239), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-18): English label 'Konstantin Tsiolkovsky', description 'Russian and Soviet rocket scientist and pioneer of the astronautic theory (1857-1935)', instance of human, born 1857 (5 or 17 September, two values recorded), died 19 September 1935, Korean label 콘스탄틴 치올콥스키. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q41239" }, { id: "src_tsiolkovsky_dsb", kind: "biography", title: "Complete Dictionary of Scientific Biography entry 'Tsiolkovsky, Konstantin Eduardovich' by A. T. Grigorian (Gale, reproduced on Encyclopedia.com; read in full including its bibliography). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: deaf from childhood and self-educated, he passed the teaching examination in 1879 and taught arithmetic and geometry; worked out a design for an all-metal dirigible and a 1894 monoplane project, built Russia's first aviation wind tunnel in 1897; set out the analytical relation between a rocket's velocity, exhaust velocity and mass in 1897 and the theory of rocket flight in 'A Study of Atmospheric Space Using Reactive Devices' (1903); later gas-driven rudders, multistage rockets and cooled combustion chambers; his ideas met indifference and he worked largely alone before 1917 and received a pension in 1921; Hermann Oberth wrote to him in 1929. Soviet-era reference biography, admiring and silent on his philosophy.", url: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/science/dictionaries-thesauruses-pictures-and-press-releases/tsiolkovsky-konstantin-eduardovich" }, { id: "src_tsiolkovsky_rsw", kind: "biography", title: "Anatoly Zak, RussianSpaceWeb 'Konstantin Tsiolkovsky' biography section, four pages read in full (Biography, Kaluga period, Tsiolkovsky and Bolshevism, Legacy), prepared with help from the Tsiolkovsky museum in Kaluga and his granddaughter. Substantive journalist-historian narrative: suspended from school at 14 and self-educated, studied in a Moscow library on black bread, built a centrifuge to test gravity effects on chickens, his 1883 'Free Space' manuscript unpublished until 1956, failed attempts to sell his airship to the military, the 1903 rocket paper in a magazine whose press run was confiscated, his letter to Oberth asserting priority, his 1926 sixteen-step plan and 'rocket trains' of 1929, his reputation as a good teacher, a Cheka arrest after the revolution, Valentin Glushko's correspondence from 1923 and a post-Soviet debate over his eugenic views. Mixes museum testimony and his own recollections; admiring but candid about limits.", url: "https://www.russianspaceweb.com/tsiolkovsky_bio.html" }, { id: "src_tsiolkovsky_siddiqi", kind: "biography", title: "Asif Siddiqi, 'Tsiolkovskii and the Invention of Russian Cosmism: Science, Mysticism, and the Conquest of Nature at the Birth of Soviet Space Exploration', chapter 6 in Science, Religion and Communism in Cold War Europe (Palgrave Macmillan, 2016; author PDF, read in full for the Tsiolkovsky sections). COUNTER-VOICE and independent scholarly source: he never joined the Communist Party; his 1903 work and its 1911-14 supplements were the first fully coherent mathematical treatment of spaceflight; the legend that Fedorov taught him is not supported by his own handwritten notes; he self-published a large body of philosophical booklets that his admirers in the 1920s and 1930s did not read; his views included eugenic and racist statements; the posthumous cult around him is a construction.", url: "https://www.asifsiddiqi.com/s/Siddiqi-Tsiolkovskii-and-the-Invention-of-Russian-Cosmism-2016.pdf" }, { id: "src_tsiolkovsky_hagemeister", kind: "biography", title: "Michael Hagemeister, 'Konstantin Tsiolkovskii and the Occult Roots of Soviet Space Travel', in The New Age of Russia: Occult and Esoteric Dimensions (Otto Sagner, 2012), pp. 135-150 (read in full from the museum-hosted PDF). Independent critical scholarship: describes the near-deaf Kaluga teacher as an autodidact who financed his research and publications out of his own pocket, was ignored by academic science, built his own network of enthusiasts, and received little state support in the 1920s despite the propaganda image; shows how his cosmic philosophy, including eugenic ideas, drew on Haeckel, Fechner and theosophy and was not original; notes that he added his signature to a letter to Stalin that probably was not his own initiative.", url: "https://www.tsiolkovsky.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/289-tsiolkovsky-hagemeister-konstantin-tsiolkovsky-and-the-occult-roots-of-soviet-space-travel-english.pdf" }, { id: "src_tsiolkovsky_rynin", kind: "biography", title: "N. A. Rynin, 'K. E. Tsiolkovskii: Life, Writings, and Rockets' (Interplanetary Flight and Communication vol. III no. 7, Leningrad 1931; NASA translation TT F-640, 1971; machine-read PDF, relevant passages read). Contemporary biography by a space-flight popularizer that includes Tsiolkovsky's own autobiographical text: his metal dirigible manuscript of 120 sheets, early-morning work before school, the Society of Technology's 1890 review of his dirigible paper, and his wind-tunnel experiments with the means at hand. Admiring and partly self-reported; its dates differ from the Dictionary of Scientific Biography for the wind tunnel.", url: "https://www.tsiolkovsky.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/292-tsiolkovsky-rynin-life-writings-and-rockets-1931-1971.pdf" }, { id: "src_tsiolkovsky_spacereview", kind: "press", title: "Taylor Dinerman, 'Studying a legend', The Space Review (16 November 2009; read in full), review of James Andrews's 'Red Cosmos: K. E. Tsiolkovskii, Grandfather of Soviet Rocketry' (Texas A&M University Press, 2009). Independent reviewer's reading: neither prophet nor fraud but a man of his time who adapted to the tsarist and then the Soviet system; was an outstanding teacher; was recast by the 1920s Soviet state as a neglected provincial genius; contains errors on dates and on his Moscow circle, so used only for the framing of his reception.", url: "https://thespacereview.com/article/1508/1" }],
