      experimentation: [66, 0.54, "s", "A"],
      // [src_tsiolkovsky_dsb, src_tsiolkovsky_rsw, src_tsiolkovsky_siddiqi, src_tsiolkovsky_hagemeister | 1883-1929 | reference biography, journalist-historian narrative, scholarly chapter, critical scholarship] The reference biography lists a metal dirigible with variable volume, a streamlined metal monoplane with a gyroscope for steering, gas-driven rudders and a cooled combustion chamber; the narrative history adds liquid oxygen and hydrogen as propellants and 'rocket trains'; the scholarly chapter treats his space mathematics as unprecedented. The same scholars note that his philosophy borrowed from Haeckel, Fechner and theosophy and that Oberth reached rocket proposals independently, so originality is strong in engineering concepts and less so in ideas generally. SELF-CALIBRATION: lowered from 74 / 0.6 because reputation as a 'father' of the field inflates how original each single idea is.
      creative_originality: [70, 0.55, "s", "A"],
      // [src_tsiolkovsky_dsb, src_tsiolkovsky_rsw, src_tsiolkovsky_siddiqi, src_tsiolkovsky_rynin | 1880s-1935 | reference biography, journalist-historian narrative, scholarly chapter, contemporary biography] Four sources document sustained, separately published work across the kinetic theory of gases, dirigible design, aerodynamics and wind-tunnel testing, aircraft design, rocket theory, astronomy, science fiction, air-cushion transport and, from 1914, a large body of philosophical booklets (68 published in his lifetime, per the scholarly chapter). Output in each field was real, though the philosophical writing is judged derivative by the scholars.
      cross_domain_range: [76, 0.64, "s", "A"],
      // [src_tsiolkovsky_dsb, src_tsiolkovsky_rsw, src_tsiolkovsky_siddiqi, src_tsiolkovsky_hagemeister | 1873-1930s | reference biography, journalist-historian narrative, scholarly chapter, critical scholarship] Self-taught after leaving school at 14, he chose his own problems and pursued them outside institutions; the reference biography says that his advanced ideas found little acceptance, the narrative history that he questioned Verne's cannon launch, and the two scholars that he built his own cosmic philosophy from selected sources and held to it against the official Marxist line until his last year. Dual-edged because the same independence produced the eugenic ideas the scholars document.
      independent_thinking: [72, 0.58, "s", "D"],
      // [src_tsiolkovsky_rsw, src_tsiolkovsky_rynin, src_tsiolkovsky_hagemeister, src_tsiolkovsky_dsb | 1873-1932 | journalist-historian narrative, contemporary biography, critical scholarship, reference biography] He taught himself in a Moscow library while spending his small allowance on books and chemicals (his recollection in the narrative history), built a centrifuge and later a wind tunnel with the means at hand (narrative history, contemporary biography), and paid for his own publications and mailed them to scientists and enthusiasts, building his own network (critical scholarship). Part of the account is his own recollection.
      resourcefulness: [70, 0.56, "s", "A"],
      // [src_tsiolkovsky_dsb, src_tsiolkovsky_siddiqi, src_tsiolkovsky_hagemeister | 1903-1935 | reference biography, scholarly chapter, critical scholarship] He wrote near the end of his life that the main motive of his life had been to move humanity forward even if only slightly (reference biography), and the scholars show that his space writing and self-published philosophy were aimed at the survival and 'perfection' of humanity, including proposals to eliminate 'defective' life that are now regarded as eugenic. The aim is stated and consistent across his writings, but the statements are his own and the content makes the impact dual-edged. CONCENTRATION CORRECTION: lowered from 70 / 0.52 (strong inference) because the motive comes mostly from his own statements and one family of scholarship.
      impact_motivation: [66, 0.46, "i", "D"],
      // [src_tsiolkovsky_rsw, src_tsiolkovsky_dsb | 1903-1929 | journalist-historian narrative, reference biography] He treated spaceflight as an interlinked system: a 1926 sixteen-step program with orbital settlements, plant-based life support and solar energy, and rocket 'trains' (multistage vehicles), alongside conditions of life for plants and animals in space in the reference biography. Two sources describe the plans but neither shows how he derived them, so the score is moderate. SELF-CALIBRATION: lowered from 66 / 0.5 and downgraded to inference because the evidence is two descriptions of plans, not shown reasoning.
