      // [src_spallanzani_dbi, src_spallanzani_dsb, src_spallanzani_biosonar | 1761-1796 | scholarly dictionary, reference biography, university group history] The Italian dictionary says he began with views favourable to Buffon and Needham and moved to ovist preformation after his experiments; the long biography says he adopted the new chemistry of the later eighteenth century and promptly accepted the ear hypothesis for bats after Jurine's result (the university group likewise says he accepted the evidence that bats rely on hearing), but it also says he welcomed a flawed experiment to protect his preformationist view and never gave spermatozoa their role. Evidence cuts both ways, so the score is near the middle and the confidence low. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 58 / 0.50 to 56 / 0.46 because the changes of view are described in one line each while the counter-example is documented in detail.
      belief_updating: [56, 0.46, "i", "D"],
      // [src_spallanzani_dsb, src_spallanzani_dbi, src_spallanzani_gale_sg | 1762-1799 | reference biography, scholarly dictionary, reference summary] He returned to the Needham dispute for some twenty years from 1761, ran hundreds of infusion trials, about 700 snail decapitations and 337 circulation experiments, climbed Etna and Stromboli at 59, descended into the crater of Vulcano and was still deep in research when he died at 70 (the Italian dictionary), discussing experiments with colleagues between bouts of coma in his last days (the long biography). Concentration check: counts and anecdotes come from one reference biography with the Italian dictionary as partial confirmation; lowered from 76 / 0.64 to 74 / 0.62.
      persistence: [74, 0.62, "s", "A"],
      // [src_spallanzani_dsb, src_spallanzani_dbi | 1773-1788 | reference biography, scholarly dictionary] He swallowed linen bags and tubes and induced vomiting to test digestion on himself, was overcome by gases just short of the crater of Etna, went to within a few feet of flowing lava, descended alone into Vulcano's crater (burned feet, staff afire) and travelled overland home from Constantinople past mountain torrents and brigands. The physical exposure is documented; none of the sources shows how he weighed it. Concentration check: the travel episodes rest on his own published travel accounts as summarised by two reference works; lowered from 72 / 0.58 to 68 / 0.54.
      risk_tolerance: [68, 0.54, "s", "D"],
      // [src_spallanzani_dsb, src_spallanzani_dbi, src_spallanzani_catholic | 1765-1795 | reference biography, scholarly dictionary, early reference entry] He disputed Needham and Buffon openly, answered John Hunter's attack in print, demanded a judicial inquiry when Pavia colleagues accused him of taking museum specimens, and afterwards published a hoax and anonymous satires against Scopoli (the dictionary and the long biography both describe this). The 1912 reprint instead says he won disputes 'with gentle courtesy', which the other two contradict; the long biography adds that he 'could become ruthlessly angry when wronged' and replied with bitterness to Volta. Concentration check: the two long sources share a documentary base and the 1912 entry conflicts with them; lowered from 76 / 0.62 to 72 / 0.58.
      conflict_tolerance: [72, 0.58, "s", "D"],
      // [src_spallanzani_dsb, src_spallanzani_dbi, src_spallanzani_britannica, src_spallanzani_gale_sg, src_spallanzani_biosonar | 1760-1798 | reference biography, scholarly dictionary, reference article, reference summary, university group history] Documented work ran across physics (the skipping of stones), hydrology (springs), microscopy and generation, regeneration, circulation, digestion, respiration and plant reproduction, volcanology, meteorology (waterspouts), marine zoology and sensory physiology (bats), plus a critique of an Italian translation of the Iliad; five source families agree on the breadth. Most of it falls inside natural history, so the score is capped below the 80s.
      cross_domain_range: [78, 0.64, "s", "A"],
      // [src_spallanzani_dsb, src_spallanzani_dbi, src_spallanzani_catholic | 1771-1788 | reference biography, scholarly dictionary, early reference entry] He took over the Pavia natural history museum from 1771 and built its collections through journeys that, by his own account to the authorities as reported in the long biography, went unrewarded, set up a small marine laboratory at Portovenere (1783), and, when Padua offered him a chair, negotiated a leave for the Constantinople journey and a salary increase from the Austrian authorities. Both long sources describe these steps; the motives are inferred from his own letters.
      proactive_agency: [66, 0.54, "s", "A"],
