    sources: [{ id: "src_musa_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Mansa Musa (Q309333), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-18): English label Mansa Musa, English description '14th-century emperor (mansa) of the Mali Empire', instance of human, born 1280 (precision year, approximate), died 1337, Korean label 만사 무사. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q309333" }, { id: "src_musa_wikipedia", kind: "wikipedia", title: "Wikipedia, 'Mansa Musa' (read in full on 2026-11-18). Orientation only: used to learn the names and the Arabic and later sources the scholarship rests on (al-Umari, Ibn Khaldun, al-Maqrizi, Ibn Battuta, the Timbuktu chronicles, and the 2019 Collet article, which was paywalled and not read), to see that the date of his birth, the story of his accession and the length of the gold-price fall are disputed, and to note that the name Kanku Musa is explained as 'Musa, son of Kanku'. Not cited for any row.", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mansa_Musa" }, { id: "src_musa_umari", kind: "archive", title: "World History Commons, 'Al-Umari's Account of Mansa Musa's Visit to Cairo' (page read in full; the same excerpt is reproduced by the Boston University African Studies Center and the University of Houston's Digital History). It prints a translated excerpt of the Syrian-born Mamluk writer al-Umari, from Levtzion and Hopkins, Corpus of Early Arabic Sources for West African History (Cambridge University Press, 1981), pp. 269-273: written some years after the 1324 visit from what Cairo residents and an emir told him, it says Musa flooded Cairo with gifts of gold, told the Mamluk emir who met him that he had come for the pilgrimage and nothing else, balked at kissing the ground before the sultan, then prostrated himself 'to God who created me' after an aide whispered to him, and that the price of gold fell in Egypt. The nearest thing to a contemporary account, but secondhand, an official's recollection, and partly admiring.", url: "https://worldhistorycommons.org/al-umaris-account-mansa-musas-visit-cairo" }, { id: "src_musa_ebsco", kind: "biography", title: "S. Sishagne, 'Mansa Musa's Pilgrimage to Mecca', EBSCO Research Starters (2022; full article read). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: reference essay presenting him as the best-known ruler of the Mali Empire (r. 1312-1337), whose 1324-1325 pilgrimage, funded by special levies on trading towns and provinces and made with a very large entourage, spread Mali's fame through North Africa, the Middle East and Europe (as early as 1375 European map-makers drew him holding a gold nugget), and who, after it, brought back books and scholars, opened Islamic schools and brought in architects who introduced a new building style; it also summarises an empire of twelve provinces under appointed governors, and says he named his son Maghan deputy during the pilgrimage. A general reference summary of the Arabic chronicles, not an independent source.", url: "https://ebsco.com/research-starters/history/mansa-musas-pilgrimage-mecca" }, { id: "src_musa_whe", kind: "biography", title: "Mark Cartwright, 'Mansa Musa I: The Richest Man in History?', World History Encyclopedia (editorially reviewed; read in full). Encyclopedia article that tells his accession in 1312, the provincial governors he appointed, the stay in Cairo (the sultan's demand that he kiss the ground, the quoted al-Maqrizi description of him as a young man with a pleasant face, the report that he and his people left Cairo in debt, and the claim that gold fell by 20 per cent and took 12 years to recover, which the fact-check page below disputes), the lodging he bought in Arabia for Mali pilgrims, the buildings and the architect credited with them after his return, and his sending of Mali scholars to Fez. Popular reference writing drawing on general histories; several figures are reported as 'in some accounts'.", url: "https://www.worldhistory.org/Mansa_Musa_I/" }, { id: "src_musa_bl", kind: "institution", title: "Eleanor Jackson, 'African kings on medieval and Renaissance maps', British Library medieval manuscripts blog (14 July 2022; read in full). Curator's account of how his 1324 pilgrimage and his gold became a European picture of West Africa: it describes the Catalan Atlas of 1375 (Paris, Bibliotheque nationale de France, MS Espagnol 30, attributed to Abraham Cresques of Majorca) as the earliest surviving map to show him, enthroned with a crown, sceptre and gold orb and captioned 'Musse Melly (Musa of Mali), lord of Guinea', the richest and noblest ruler of the region because of its gold, and shows later maps repeating the emphasis on his wealth.", url: "https://www.bl.uk/stories/blogs/posts/african-kings-on-medieval-and-renaissance-maps" }, { id: "src_musa_northwestern", kind: "press", title: "Stephanie Kulke, 'A Golden Age: King Mansa Musa's Reign', Northwestern Magazine (Spring 2019; read in full), on the Block Museum exhibition Caravans of Gold. Museum-linked feature: repeats al-Umari's account of the gifts that depressed the price of gold, describes the pilgrimage as one of the great Sahara caravans, and relays the exhibition speaker Gus Casely-Hayford's view that Musa 'sought knowledge' and brought scholars and an Andalusian architect back to build the Djinguereber mosque. Exhibition publicity, so celebratory, and the figures given (8,000 courtiers, 12,000 servants) come from the speaker.", url: "https://magazine.northwestern.edu/features/caravans-of-gold-fragments-in-time/a-golden-age-king-mansa-musas-reign" }, { id: "src_musa_factcheck", kind: "press", title: "Aris Manus, 'Fact Check: $400 Billion Mansa Musa Fortune Traces to a 2012 Website List', Ancient Origins (October 2026; read in full). Critical check of the popular claims, drawing on the historian Warren Schultz (2006): says the hajj, the gifts of gold and a fall of gold against silver in Cairo are established; al-Umari wrote at second hand and about twelve years later, and was not in Egypt in 1324; three later Mamluk writers also record a fall, but only al-Umari says it lasted twelve years, which Schultz calls unsupportable; the 'richest person ever' and $400 billion claims have no historical basis; and Ibn Battuta's material on Musa, gathered in 1352-1353 from a governor who had travelled with him, is also secondhand. A skeptical outlet whose fact-check leans on one historian.", url: "https://www.ancient-origins.net/news-history-archaeology/mansa-musa-400-billion-richest-man-fact-check-00103294" }],
