    sources: [{ id: "src_paganini_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Niccolo Paganini (Q66075), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-18): English label 'Niccolo Paganini', description 'Italian violinist and composer (1782-1840)', instance of human, born 27 October 1782 (Wikidata also carries 1784 values), died 27 May 1840 (also a 17 May value), Korean label 니콜로 파가니니. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q66075" }, { id: "src_paganini_dbi", kind: "biography", title: "Roberto Grisley, 'Paganini, Niccolo', Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani vol. 80 (Treccani, 2014; read in full, in Italian). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: scholarly national-biography entry built on the correspondence and archival record; the Milan concert of 29 October 1813 as the true start of his career, the 24 Capricci (contract with Ricordi in 1817, published 1820, never played by him in public), the five violin concertos, the variation sets, his instrumental techniques (harmonics, tremolo with melody, scordatura, work on the single string) and his place in the line of Viotti; his European tour of 1828-34 (Vienna, Berlin, Warsaw, Paris, London); the Lucca court post from 1805 and its end; his legal and financial troubles including a gambling-debt complaint at Venice (1817) and the bankruptcy of the Casino Paganini in Paris (1837-38); his short, contested direction of the Parma ducal orchestra (1836); the gift of 20,000 francs to Berlioz (1838); the refusal of church burial; and a frank discussion of how anecdote and his own press management built his fame. Scholarly, balanced, drawing on letters.", url: "https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/niccolo-paganini_(Dizionario-Biografico)/" }, { id: "src_paganini_baker", kind: "biography", title: "Nicolas Slonimsky, Laura Kuhn and Dennis McIntire, 'Paganini, Niccolo', Baker's Biographical Dictionary of Musicians (Gale, reproduced on Encyclopedia.com; read in full). Music-reference biography: his teachers, the Lucca court posts and his break with the court in December 1809, the Milan concerts of 1813 that brought national prominence, the displacement of the rivals Lafont (Milan, 1816) and Lipinski (Piacenza, 1818), the tour from Vienna in 1828, the Paris and London debuts of 1831, the decline of his health from 1834, the loss of his voice in 1838 and death at Nice, the technical tricks he used (retuning the A string a semitone up, playing the Witches' Dance on one string), and the 24 Caprices, Moto perpetuo and concertos as his outstanding works. Standard reference, partly repeating older biographical tradition.", url: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/arts/dictionaries-thesauruses-pictures-and-press-releases/paganini-niccolo-0" }, { id: "src_paganini_gooley", kind: "biography", title: "Dana Gooley, 'Paganini, Niccolo', Encyclopedia of Modern Europe: Europe 1789-1914 (Gale, reproduced on Encyclopedia.com; read in full). Scholarly reference entry by a historian of virtuosity: the most successful concert career of any instrumentalist before him, the Caprices (dedicated 'to the artists', treated by him as studies and not played in public) as a foundation of violin technique, the Sonata Napoleone for the G string alone, his break from court life as temperamentally unsuited to its restrictions, itinerant concert life interspersed with periods of inactivity through illness and gambling, the tours from 1828 with only his son and an occasional travelling companion who kept his books, how journalists built the 'demonic' image, the failed Casino Paganini, and his technical innovations (harmonics, left-hand pizzicato, mixed textures, G-string composition) and successors. Independent scholarly synthesis.", url: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/history/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/paganini-niccolo" }, { id: "src_paganini_mallery", kind: "biography", title: "Richard L. Mallery, 'Niccolo Paganini', EBSCO Research Starters (2024; read in full). General-reader biography that leans on Courcy's classic life: his father's demanding training, his move to freelance life at Lucca in 1801, early gambling and the pawned violin, the Milan concerts of 1813, the tours from 1828 with ticket prices twice the usual, the Casino Paganini lawsuits and his death at Nice, and the argument that he defined the role of the freelance concert musician; it also records criticism that he pandered to popular taste. Popular secondary source, partly dependent on one earlier biography.", url: "https://ebsco.com/research-starters/history/niccolo-paganini" }, { id: "src_paganini_stratton", kind: "biography", title: "Stephen S. Stratton, 'Nicolo Paganini: His Life and Work' (London: The Strad Library, 1907; Project Gutenberg text, read in full for the biographical chapters). Early English biography drawing on Fetis, the 1830-31 press and Berlioz's memoirs: his practice of single passages for many hours, his own account of how the Lucca court led him to play on fewer strings and to compose the 'Napoleon' sonata for the G string, the London price dispute and his published letter of June 1831, the Casino Paganini lawsuits of 1839-40 (damages of 50,000 francs on appeal), and Berlioz's account of Paganini's visit to him in 1834 and of the gesture and gift after the concert of December 1838. Dated, anecdotal and partly based on his own statements and one memoir.", url: "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/39571" }, { id: "src_paganini_kawabata", kind: "institution", title: "Maiko Kawabata, 'What does Paganini mean to us today?' (Royal College of Music, 2018 exhibition essay; read in full). Modern scholar's short essay: Paganini's habit of improvising and 'preluding' rather than playing note-perfect, the physicality of his stage manner, his instruments, and the view that being the 'demonic' virtuoso was central to his identity (developed in her 2013 book). Contains personal opinion about modern guitarists and violinists and a closing ethical comment on his private conduct, which is not used here. Used only for the point about improvisation and performance style.", url: "https://researchonline.rcm.ac.uk/id/eprint/340/1/What%20does%20Paganini%20mean%20to%20us%20today_.pdf" }],
