      // [src_szymborska_nobel, src_szymborska_culture_bio, src_szymborska_rusinek_memoir, src_szymborska_cavanagh | 1945-2012 | award-body biography, cultural-institute profile, secretary's memoir, translator's introduction] Beyond poetry she translated French poetry and wrote a long-running weekly column of short prose pieces on books (Nobel Foundation note), illustrated a textbook and studied literature and then sociology (Culture.pl), made collages over four decades (Rusinek) and wrote the 'Literary Mailbox' advice column (Cavanagh). All of these sit within writing, editing and the visual-literary arts, so the range is modest.
      cross_domain_range: [58, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_szymborska_culture_bio, src_szymborska_culture_obit, src_szymborska_nation | 1952-1957 | cultural-institute profile, press obituary, critic's essay] Her first two volumes (1952, 1954) were written in the style the political era required and she left them out of later selections, answering a question about them that she had been 'young, naive and clueless'; the 1957 volume Calling Out to Yeti moved away from committed poetry toward philosophical distance, and The Nation describes a brief socialist-realist phase followed by irony and skepticism of grand schemes. Her party and political activity is context and is not scored. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 72 / 0.56 to 68 / 0.52 because the two Culture.pl pieces share one lineage (Illg's conversation and her friends), and her stated reason is a self-justification.
      belief_updating: [68, 0.52, "s", "A"],
      // [src_szymborska_culture_obit, src_szymborska_rusinek_interview, src_szymborska_rusinek_memoir, src_szymborska_nobel | 1957-2012 | press obituary, secretary interview, secretary memoir, award-body lecture] The obituary describes a language kept at a distance from ideologies, philosophical systems and stereotypes; her secretary says she disliked anything done collectively (associated with the communist era), refused to let her name be used as the 'engine' of initiatives and followed her own path; in her lecture she contrasts people who 'know' with the value of saying 'I don't know'. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 70 / 0.54 to 66 / 0.50 because the strongest sentences are an obituary summary and her own lecture, and the secretary's evidence is one family.
      independent_thinking: [66, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_szymborska_rusinek_memoir, src_szymborska_rusinek_interview, src_szymborska_culture_bio | 1996-2012 | secretary memoir, secretary interview, cultural-institute profile] After the Nobel she kept an unlisted number, answered invitations with the 'poetics of the negative response', declined interviews for the Polish press, refused honorary roles, asked to be left alone for several days at a time to make collages and said she wanted to be a person, not a 'personage'; her friends recall that she enjoyed calm and disliked questions about her private life. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 74 / 0.56 to 70 / 0.52 because the post-Nobel detail comes almost entirely from one secretary's two accounts, and some of the refusals respond to an overload of requests after the prize.
      autonomy_need: [70, 0.52, "s", "A"],
      // [src_szymborska_nobel, src_szymborska_rusinek_memoir | 1996 | award-body lecture and speech, secretary memoir] In her Nobel Lecture she says she values the phrase 'I don't know' and that each poem is a provisional answer; the presentation speech says her poems approach 'a final enigma'; her secretary recalls that she told journalists a question was good but she needed time to think and asked them to come back in the autumn. Mostly her own statement with one anecdote, so it is held at inference level. Kept after review.
